---
/**
 * Why a beta-blocker breaks the heart-rate bands, and the 1-to-10 scale used
 * instead. Rendered by /science/movement/effort; see scienceTopics.ts.
 *
 * Laid out as the canvas board (WebEffort): two panels, the problem, the
 * scale and one effort per session on the left, how the scales line up and
 * why these cut-points on the right; then the row of links; then what
 * switches over and what does not.
 */
import CitationCard from "../../components/CitationCard.astro";
import { EFFORT_SECTION } from "../../data/science";
import { topicHref } from "../../data/scienceTopics";
const zone2 = topicHref("zone-2");
const volume = topicHref("weekly-volume");
const workout = topicHref("a-workout");
const testCrf = topicHref("test-crf");
const acsm = EFFORT_SECTION[0].url;

/** Apple's four words, Verve's three bands, and the ACSM scales they line up with. */
const mapping: Array<[string, string, string, string]> = [
  ["1–3", "Easy", "light", "Borg CR10 under 3 · RPE under 12"],
  ["4–6", "Moderate", "moderate", "Borg CR10 3–4 · RPE 12–13"],
  ["7–8", "Hard", "vigorous", "Borg CR10 5 and up · RPE 14 and up"],
  ["9–10", "All out", "vigorous", "Borg CR10 5 and up · RPE 14 and up"],
];
---

<p class="mt-[18px] text-base leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  Why a beta-blocker breaks the heart-rate bands, the 1-to-10 effort scale
  Verve uses instead, and how it maps to the guideline scales. Everything on
  the <a href={workout}>workout page</a> that says how hard a session was is
  read from heart rate: the three bands, the Zone 2 minutes, the moderate and
  vigorous minutes that climb the Weekly Ascent. For most people that is the
  right instrument. For a person on a beta-blocker it is the wrong one.
</p>

<div class="mt-5 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
  <!-- the problem, the scale, one effort per session -->
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-3.5 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:p-6">
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">The problem.</strong>
      Beta-blockers blunt the heart-rate response to exertion: at a given
      workload the rate runs roughly 20 to 30 bpm lower than it would
      unmedicated, and the peak rate is held down the same way. The effort is
      real and the body is working as hard; the heart is simply not reporting
      it. So every band drawn from heart rate undercounts: a walk that would
      have sat in the middle of the moderate band reads as light, an interval
      session that would have been vigorous reads as moderate, the Zone 2
      minutes are a fraction of the real ones, and the week undercounts.
      Re-drawing the bands lower does not fix it, because the blunting is not
      a fixed offset and the dose changes. ACSM&rsquo;s guidelines say the
      same: on rate-limiting medication, prescribe and judge intensity by
      perceived exertion, not by heart rate.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">The scale.</strong>
      From watchOS 11 and iOS 18, Apple Watch asks for an effort rating when
      a workout ends: 1 to 10, with Easy, Moderate, Hard and All out as the
      four words along it. The rating is stored in Apple Health as the Workout
      Effort Score, and for a session that was not rated Apple can store an
      estimated score of its own. Verve reads the rating as it is, with no
      smoothing and no re-scoring from heart rate, and the card says which
      kind it was: rated by you, or estimated. A watch that does not ask, or
      an older one, gets a row on the workout page where the rating can be
      typed, same scale. The scale is Borg&rsquo;s: the perceived strain
      tracks the physiological strain closely enough to stand in for it,
      which is the whole reason a number typed after the session can be a
      measure.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">One effort per session.</strong>
      Heart rate gives a minute-by-minute split; a rating gives one number
      for the whole session. So a 32-minute walk rated 6 is 32 moderate
      minutes; rated 8, it is 32 vigorous minutes. There is no warm-up
      discounted, no hard middle picked out, no cool-down trimmed. Coarser,
      and honest about being coarser.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- how the scales line up, and why these cut-points -->
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-3.5 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-5 sm:p-6">
    <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-text-tertiary">
      How the scales line up
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] text-[12.5px]">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-left font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-text-tertiary">
            <th class="px-2 py-2 font-semibold">Apple effort</th>
            <th class="px-2 py-2 font-semibold">Apple&rsquo;s word</th>
            <th class="px-2 py-2 font-semibold">Verve band</th>
            <th class="px-2 py-2 font-semibold">ACSM equivalents</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/[0.06]">
          {mapping.map(([a, w, v, acsmEq]) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="px-2 py-2 font-mono font-semibold text-text-primary">{a}</td>
              <td class="px-2 py-2 text-text-secondary">{w}</td>
              <td class={`px-2 py-2 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider ${v === "vigorous" ? "text-danger" : v === "moderate" ? "text-met-hero" : "text-success-light"}`}>{v}</td>
              <td class="px-2 py-2 font-mono text-text-secondary">{acsmEq}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
      <strong class="text-text-primary">Why these cut-points.</strong>
      ACSM places moderate at Borg CR10 3–4 (RPE 12–13) and vigorous at 5 and
      above (RPE 14 and above). Apple&rsquo;s own labels put Moderate at 4–6
      and Hard at 7–8; Verve keeps Apple&rsquo;s words so the watch and the
      app never disagree about a number the person just typed. Apple&rsquo;s
      four words are mapped onto the ACSM bands; nothing finer than that is
      claimed.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- the row of links -->
<div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1.5 font-mono text-[11px] text-accent-light">
  <a href={zone2} class="hover:text-text-primary">&rarr; Zone 2</a>
  <a href={volume} class="hover:text-text-primary">&rarr; Weekly volume</a>
  <a href={testCrf} class="hover:text-text-primary">&rarr; Test your CRF on a beta-blocker</a>
  <a href={acsm} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="hover:text-text-primary">&rarr; ACSM Guidelines, 11th ed.</a>
</div>

<div class="mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-white/8 bg-white/4 p-6">
  <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What switches over.</strong>
    When the profile says beta-blocker, the minutes, the <a href={zone2}>Zone
    2</a> total and the <a href={volume}>Weekly Ascent</a> are counted by
    effort instead of heart rate: a session rated 4 to 6 is a Zone 2 session
    in full, 7 and above climbs the Ascent as vigorous. The heart-rate trace
    is still drawn on the workout page, because it is what the watch
    measured, but nothing is graded from it. Strength, flexibility and
    balance are not affected: they were never graded by heart rate, and
    they still are not.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
    <strong class="text-text-primary">What does not switch over.</strong>
    The resting heart rate tile, one-minute recovery and night HRV are read
    against your own band as before. A beta-blocker lowers all three
    baselines, and the band moves with them, which is the point of a band
    that is yours. What the medication changes is the response to effort,
    and that is the only place the heart-rate reading has been retired.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
  {EFFORT_SECTION.map((c) => <CitationCard {...c} />)}
</div>
